// Scope: persistent per-function symbol table, with LEXICAL BLOCK SCOPING.
//
// The parser builds this as it emits ops; when a function finishes parsing the
// final `Scope` is handed to the `IrFunction` for downstream consumers (the
// Maxon→Std lowerer and the future LSP name-resolution query).
//
// A block (`if` / `else` / `while` body) opens a FRAME: every declaration inside
// it is torn down at the block's `end`, so `if c 'b' … let x = 1 … end 'b'`
// followed by a reference to `x` is a name-resolution failure, not a live binding
// (specs/block-scoping.md). Frames are MARKS into flat declaration stacks, not a
// stack of per-frame arrays: pushScope pushes the current stack length — O(1),
// and with NO heap object per frame, which matters because a block is the most
// numerous scope in any real program — and popScope drains back to it, still
// O(declarations-in-this-frame). An EMPTY mark stack means function scope:
// declarations there live for the whole function and `reset` tears them down.
//
// SHADOWING IS LEGAL, and it is why a frame records more than names. `vars` is
// keyed by name, so an inner `let x` OVERWRITES the outer `x`'s entry; simply
// REMOVING the key at scope exit would delete the outer binding too, and
// `let x = 1 / if c 'b' let x = 2 end 'b' / return x` would report `x` undefined
// instead of returning 1. So a declaration that DISPLACES an existing binding
// pushes the displaced `VarInfo` onto `shadowedInfos`, and popScope drains the
// frame in REVERSE — putting each displaced binding back — which also unwinds a
// name declared twice within one frame through its own chain.
//
// BINDINGS ARE MUTATED IN PLACE, NOT REPLACED. `VarInfo` is a heap `type`, so
// `vars` stores a reference; `setValue` writes `boundValue` through it. That is
// what lets the parser hold a DIRECT reference to a mutable binding (its loop-phi
// list and its merge snapshots do — see Parser.mutableVars) and still address the
// right binding when a nested block shadows its NAME. A name-keyed rebind cannot:
// it would always resolve to the innermost binding, so a `break` out of a block
// that shadows a loop-carried `var` would carry the SHADOW's value to the loop's
// exit phi — a silent miscompile.
//
// OWNERSHIP SCAFFOLD (M1, inert): the Own tier (M6) places `own.drop`/`own.release`
// at scope exit for every NON-trivial (owned/borrow/shared) binding a frame declared
// — the deterministic static-drop mechanism that replaces v1's late liveness-driven
// refcount reclamation. `popScope` is where that happens, and it needs no side list to
// do it: draining a frame ALREADY resolves each of its declarations back to the
// `VarInfo` being torn down, and that record carries both the `ownership` kind to test
// and the type + slot a drop has to name. A parallel "owned names" array would be a
// strict projection of `vars` kept in lockstep by hand — state that can drift, buying
// nothing. At M1 every binding is `trivial`, so the hook never fires.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE SLOT HALF OF THIS API HAS NO CALLERS, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT OF THIS PARAGRAPH.**
// Measured at P1.1a wave 3: `declare`, `declareShadowing`, `bindExistingSlot`, `installVerbatim`,
// `lookup`, `getType`, `allVars`, `nextSlot` and `setNextSlot` are called from nowhere outside this
// file. Everything the parser actually binds goes through `declareValueBinding` (the M2
// SSA-substitution model), its payload/parameter siblings, or `declareSelfField` (which is slotless by
// construction).
//
// **The consumers, named rather than implied:** all of them are the mutable/`mem2reg` model, whose
// rung is not scheduled and whose shape is not settled.
//
// ⚠ **AND THAT GROUP IS OWED A HARDER LOOK THAN "IT WILL BE USED LATER", BECAUSE TWO RUNGS HAVE NOW
// DISPROVED ITS SIBLINGS.** `VarInfo.fieldOffset` was scaffolded beside these at M1, for the
// self-field alias, with a comment explaining why an offset was the right thing to carry. When the
// consumer finally arrived — P1.1b — the right answer turned out to be the field's INDEX, and
// the scaffold was not extended but DELETED: an offset would have forced a second emit site and
// left the mutability check no home. **`declarePayloadBinding`/`declarePayloadBindingShadowing` went
// the same way, and they were the ones this paragraph named a consumer for**: the rung that finally
// wrote to a payload (`mutable-enums`) needed a VALUE binding keyed on the scrutinee's `ValueId`, not
// a SLOT binding keyed on its name, so the scaffold was deleted rather than called and
// `declarePayloadValueBinding` written against the model shv2 actually has. The scaffold did not save
// either rung any work; both had to undo it first. That is the `mintPhi` trap's other face, and it is
// direct evidence about THESE members, not an analogy — they were guessed at by the same commit, for
// the same tier, on the same reasoning. Deleting them is real work with a real diff (it reaches
// `VarInfo.slot`,
// `IrFunction.varSlotCount` and the Maxon→Std lowering's comments) and is deliberately NOT smuggled
// into a review commit; it is its own change, with the full suite as its gate. Until it lands, no
// one should read this API as a design that has been agreed — it is a guess that is still waiting to
// find out whether it was right, and its sibling was not.

export union ScopeLookupError implements Error
	// String, not the raw slice: this arm exists only to be FORMATTED into a diagnostic.
	notFound(name String)
end 'ScopeLookupError'

// Raised when the name being bound would overwrite a self-field alias registered at
// instance-method entry (E3006). Scope is the only layer that sees both the alias and the
// colliding declaration, so detection lives here.
//
// `declareBinding` is the live raiser — it is the one path a parameter, a body local and a match-arm
// payload binding are all bound through, so it covers both shapes the bootstrap reports. (`declare`
// carries the same guard for the slot-based model; it has no caller today — see `declareShadowing`.)
export union ScopeError implements Error
	// String, not the raw slice: this arm exists only to be FORMATTED into a diagnostic.
	shadowsSelfField(name String)
end 'ScopeError'

// A frame boundary: the height of the declaration stack when the frame was
// opened. Wide range — declaration counts are unbounded.
// ⚠ NOT exported: a frame mark never leaves the scope stack, and shv2's own `checkUnusedExports`
// (E3092) is what says so — the SELF-COMPILE runs that pass and the bootstrap does not.
typealias ScopeMark = int(0 to u64.max)
typealias ScopeMarkArray = Array with ScopeMark

export type Scope
	var vars as VarInfoMap
	// Every declaration made while a frame was open, in declaration order —
	// innermost frame on top. Drained back to a frame's mark by popScope.
	var declNames as ByteArrayArray
	// Parallel to `declNames`: did this declaration DISPLACE an existing binding of
	// the same name? A dense bool column rather than an optional `VarInfo` per
	// declaration, so the common (non-shadowing) case costs no heap object.
	var declShadowed as BoolArray
	// The displaced bindings, pushed only by the declarations `declShadowed` marks.
	// popScope pops one per marked declaration, in reverse, and re-installs it.
	var shadowedInfos as VarInfoArray
	// One entry per open frame: the `declNames` height at pushScope.
	var frameMarks as ScopeMarkArray
	export var nextSlot as VarSlot

	export static function create() returns Scope
		return Self{
			vars: VarInfoMap.create(),
			declNames: ByteArrayArray.create(),
			declShadowed: BoolArray.create(),
			shadowedInfos: VarInfoArray.create(),
			frameMarks: ScopeMarkArray.create(),
			nextSlot: 0
		}
	end 'create'

	// Reset the scope for a new function parse.
	export function reset()
		vars = VarInfoMap.create()
		declNames = ByteArrayArray.create()
		declShadowed = BoolArray.create()
		shadowedInfos = VarInfoArray.create()
		frameMarks = ScopeMarkArray.create()
		nextSlot = 0
	end 'reset'

	// Declare a variable and return its slot. Throws
	// `ScopeError.shadowsSelfField` when `name` currently maps to a self-field
	// alias (binding a local of the same name would overwrite it — E3006).
	export function declare(name ByteArray, varType MaxonType, mutable bool) returns VarSlot throws ScopeError
		if self.isSelfField(name) 'shadow'
			throw ScopeError.shadowsSelfField(String.from(name))
		end 'shadow'
		return self.declareShadowing(name, varType: varType, mutable: mutable)
	end 'declare'

	// Allocate a slot for `name` without the self-field-shadow guard — the shared slot-allocating
	// core for `declare`.
	//
	// ⚠ It also called itself "the error-recovery path after a caller reported E3006" until P1.1a
	// wave 3's review, and there is no such caller and never has been: the E3006 that exists is
	// `declareValueBinding`'s, and the parser turns it into a `ParseError` and stops rather than
	// recovering. Saying so mattered because that sentence was the only thing making this member
	// look consumed. See the header — it is not.
	export function declareShadowing(name ByteArray, varType MaxonType, mutable bool) returns VarSlot
		let slot = nextSlot
		nextSlot = nextSlot + 1
		self.install(VarInfo.create(name, varType: varType, mutable: mutable, slot: slot))
		return slot
	end 'declareShadowing'

	// Bind `name` to an ALREADY-ALLOCATED slot (no new slot). Aliases a user
	// binding onto a shared compiler slot so a single scope-exit release frees
	// it (a separate slot would double-free).
	export function bindExistingSlot(name ByteArray, slot VarSlot, varType MaxonType, mutable bool)
		self.install(VarInfo.create(name, varType: varType, mutable: mutable, slot: slot))
	end 'bindExistingSlot'

	// Declare a self-field alias — field `fieldIndex` of the enclosing type, bound by name at
	// instance-method entry so a bare `count` inside the method resolves to `self.count`.
	//
	// It allocates NO slot and returns nothing. A self-field alias is neither of the two things a
	// slot is for: it holds no local storage (a read is a `loadIndirect` through the receiver) and
	// aliases no SSA value (a write is a `storeIndirect`, not a rebind). It exists to occupy the
	// NAME — so that a read resolves through it, and so that a local or a parameter trying to take
	// the same name is caught below rather than silently displacing the field.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT DOES NOT GUARD, AND THE ONE GUARD IS `declareValueBinding`'s.** The aliases are
	// declared into a freshly `reset` scope BEFORE the parameters and before any body local, so
	// nothing can be here to displace: every name that could collide with a field arrives LATER,
	// through `declareValueBinding`, which is the single path a parameter and a local are both
	// bound by. Guarding from this side too would be the same rule asked twice — and the two sides
	// would disagree about WHERE to point, because the token worth naming is always the colliding
	// declaration's, which only the other side holds. Ordering the declarations is what makes one
	// guard sufficient; both cases the bootstrap reports (a parameter at `4:16`, a body local at
	// `5:7`) come out of that one site, each anchored on the token its own caller has.
	//
	// ⚠ Two fields of ONE name therefore overwrite here rather than reporting. That is the
	// pre-existing duplicate-field gap, not a new one: `StructLayout.addField` accepts the second
	// declaration and `indexOfField` resolves every use to the first, and the BOOTSTRAP accepts
	// the same program (measured: `type Dup` with two `var x` compiles and runs). Inventing E3006
	// for it here would reject a program the oracle accepts, on a rung with no case for it; it
	// wants its own diagnostic against the LAYOUT, where both compilers are silent.
	export function declareSelfField(name ByteArray, varType MaxonType, fieldIndex FieldIndex)
		self.install(VarInfo.createSelfField(name, varType: varType, fieldIndex: fieldIndex))
	end 'declareSelfField'

	// Declare a MATCH-ARM PAYLOAD binding. A value binding like any other — reads resolve to the SSA
	// value the arm loaded out of the box — carrying the box coordinates a WRITE goes back through
	// (`VarInfo.isPayloadBinding`, `Parser.writeBackPayload`). It shares `declareBinding` with the two
	// constructors below, so the self-field shadow rule is the one rule for every name a scope installs.
	export function declarePayloadValueBinding(name ByteArray, varType MaxonType, mutable bool, value ValueId, scrutBox ValueId, slotOffset ByteOffset, storage MaxonType) returns VarInfo throws ScopeError
		return try self.declareBinding(VarInfo.createPayloadBinding(name, varType: varType, mutable: mutable, boundValue: value, scrutBox: scrutBox, slotOffset: slotOffset, storage: storage))
	end 'declarePayloadValueBinding'

	// Declare a value binding (M2's SSA-substitution model): bind `name` to the SSA
	// value `value`, so a later reference resolves to it via `lookupValue`. No slot
	// is allocated — value bindings need no stack storage (contrast the slot-based
	// `declare*` paths that back the future mutable/mem2reg model).
	//
	// ⭐ **THE SELF-FIELD GUARD IS NOW ON THIS PATH, AND THIS IS THE RUNG ITS ABSENCE NAMED.** It
	// used to say the guard was "unnecessary here: M2 has no instance methods, so no self-field
	// alias can exist to shadow — that guard joins this path when methods land." Methods have
	// landed, and this is the path every local and every parameter is bound through, so it is the
	// path an alias can now be displaced on: `let count = 5` inside a method of a `type Counter`
	// that declares `count`. `vars` is keyed by NAME, so that `let` would overwrite the alias and
	// every later `count` in the method would read the LOCAL — silently, and the field's own reads
	// and writes would simply stop happening. E3006, and the bootstrap agrees down to the
	// reasoning ("local 'count' shadows self field 'Counter.count' — rename the local to avoid
	// silent type confusion at every read/write keyed on the name").
	//
	// Returns the `VarInfo` it installed, because a `var` binding is tracked BY
	// REFERENCE (see the header): the parser keeps mutable bindings in its loop-phi
	// list as the objects themselves, so a nested block that shadows the NAME cannot
	// misdirect a phi write to the shadow.
	export function declareValueBinding(name ByteArray, varType MaxonType, mutable bool, value ValueId, brandId TypeNameId) returns VarInfo throws ScopeError
		return try self.declareBinding(VarInfo.createValueBinding(name, varType: varType, mutable: mutable, boundValue: value, brandId: brandId))
	end 'declareValueBinding'

	// Declare a PARAMETER's value binding. Identical to `declareValueBinding` in everything the scope
	// does with it, and different in the one bit the binding carries (`VarInfo.isParameter`), which is
	// what tells "may this name be rebound?" from "may the container it denotes be written?".
	export function declareParameterBinding(name ByteArray, varType MaxonType, mutable bool, value ValueId, brandId TypeNameId) returns VarInfo throws ScopeError
		return try self.declareBinding(VarInfo.createParameterBinding(name, varType: varType, mutable: mutable, boundValue: value, brandId: brandId))
	end 'declareParameterBinding'

	// Declare a CELL-RESIDENT binding (PBR-2): `name` denotes the 8-byte heap cell `cell`, so a read is a
	// `loadIndirect` through it and a rebind a `storeIndirect` — see `VarInfo.isCellBinding`. A named sibling
	// to `declarePayloadValueBinding` rather than fields set on a returned `VarInfo`, because a binding that
	// was installed as one kind and mutated into another is a kind no single site declares, and the parser's
	// merge snapshots and loop-phi list hold these objects BY REFERENCE.
	//
	// It shares `declareBinding` with the three above, so the self-field shadow rule stays the one rule for
	// every name a scope installs.
	export function declareCellBinding(name ByteArray, varType MaxonType, mutable bool, cell ValueId, isParameter bool, brandId TypeNameId) returns VarInfo throws ScopeError
		return try self.declareBinding(VarInfo.createCellBinding(name, varType: varType, mutable: mutable, cell: cell, isParameter: isParameter, brandId: brandId))
	end 'declareCellBinding'

	// The shared body of the four value-binding declarations above: refuse a name that would shadow a
	// self-field alias, then install. One home, so a local, a parameter, a match-arm payload binding and a
	// cell-resident binding cannot come to disagree about whether shadowing a field is legal.
	function declareBinding(info VarInfo) returns VarInfo throws ScopeError
		if self.isSelfField(info.name) 'shadow'
			throw ScopeError.shadowsSelfField(String.from(info.name))
		end 'shadow'
		self.install(info)
		return info
	end 'declareBinding'

	// THE single funnel for every declare* path: install the binding in `vars`, and —
	// when a frame is open — record it for O(decls) teardown, capturing the binding it
	// DISPLACES so popScope can put that one back.
	//
	// With NO frame open the declaration is at function scope: it lives for the whole
	// function and is torn down by `reset`, so it needs no frame bookkeeping and can
	// never be shadowed-then-restored (it IS the outermost binding of its name).
	function install(info VarInfo)
		if frameMarks.count() == 0 'functionScope'
			vars.upsert(info.name, value: info)
			return
		end 'functionScope'

		var shadowed = false
		if let displaced = try vars.get(info.name) 'displaces'
			shadowedInfos.push(displaced)
			shadowed = true
		end 'displaces'
		declNames.push(info.name)
		declShadowed.push(shadowed)
		vars.upsert(info.name, value: info)
	end 'install'

	// Rebind an ALREADY-DECLARED name to a new SSA value (M4b's on-the-fly SSA): a
	// `var` reassignment (`x = expr`). Reads (`lookupValue`) resolve to this current
	// value, so a read after a reassignment sees the new value — `boundValue` IS the
	// variable's current SSA version, not the initializer's. Written THROUGH the
	// binding (a heap reference), so every holder of that `VarInfo` — the parser's
	// loop-phi list, a merge snapshot — observes it. Panics on an unknown name: every
	// caller has already established the binding exists (a `var` decl, or a mutability
	// check that fetched the VarInfo).
	//
	// Name-keyed, so it always hits the INNERMOST binding — which is exactly right for
	// a source-level assignment. The parser's phi machinery deliberately does NOT come
	// through here; it writes `boundValue` on the binding it holds (see the header).
	export function setValue(name ByteArray, value ValueId)
		var info = try vars.get(name) otherwise panic("Scope.setValue: '{String.from(name)}' not declared — caller must establish the binding before rebinding it")
		info.boundValue = value
	end 'setValue'

	// Resolve a value binding to the SSA value it aliases. Throws
	// `ScopeLookupError.notFound` for an unbound name; the parser turns that into a
	// positioned diagnostic (an undefined identifier in an expression).
	export function lookupValue(name ByteArray) returns ValueId throws ScopeLookupError
		let info = try self.lookupInfoOrThrow(name)
		return info.boundValue
	end 'lookupValue'

	// Look up a variable by name. Throws ScopeLookupError.notFound if absent.
	export function lookup(name ByteArray) returns VarSlot throws ScopeLookupError
		let info = try self.lookupInfoOrThrow(name)
		return info.slot
	end 'lookup'

	// The throwing VarInfo fetch shared by `lookup`/`lookupValue`: an unbound name
	// is a name-resolution failure (ScopeLookupError.notFound) the parser turns into
	// a positioned diagnostic. `getInfo`/`isSelfField` deliberately keep their own
	// fetch — they recover differently (panic / false), not by throwing this arm.
	function lookupInfoOrThrow(name ByteArray) returns VarInfo throws ScopeLookupError
		return try vars.get(name) otherwise throw ScopeLookupError.notFound(String.from(name))
	end 'lookupInfoOrThrow'

	// Get the VarInfo for a variable, or panic if not found.
	export function getInfo(name ByteArray) returns VarInfo
		return try vars.get(name) otherwise panic("Scope: undefined variable '{String.from(name)}'")
	end 'getInfo'

	// Check if a variable is defined.
	export function contains(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return vars.contains(name)
	end 'contains'

	// Read-only iteration over the per-function symbol table. Returned map is
	// the live backing store — callers MUST treat it as read-only.
	export function allVars() returns VarInfoMap
		return vars
	end 'allVars'

	// True when `name` is a self-field alias inside an instance method. Returns false for unknown
	// names so callers can probe without checking `contains()`.
	//
	// ⭐ **ONE PREDICATE, TWO SIDES OF ONE RULE, AND THEY MUST NEVER DISAGREE.** It is asked by the
	// READER — `Parser.parseVariableReference`, deciding whether a bare `count` lowers to a
	// `loadIndirect` through the receiver — and by every WRITER, the three `declare*` paths asking
	// whether a new binding would displace an alias (E3006). Those are the same question wearing
	// two grammatical moods, not two questions.
	//
	// ⚠ It was written TWICE until this rung's review: a private `wouldShadowSelfField` with a
	// byte-identical body sat 180 lines above, serving the writers while this one served the
	// reader. Nothing made them agree — and had they drifted, NEITHER side would have failed to
	// compile: a writer answering `false` lets a local silently displace the field, so every later
	// read in the method resolves to the local and the field's reads and writes simply stop
	// happening; a reader answering `false` sends the bare name to `lookupValue`, which hands back
	// an alias's unset `boundValue` of 0 — the RECEIVER. Both are wrong answers with no diagnostic.
	// (v1 carries the same two functions, `Scope.maxon:79` and `:199`; the split was PORTED, not
	// reasoned. A copy needs a reason as much as a divergence does.)
	export function isSelfField(name ByteArray) returns bool
		let info = try vars.get(name) otherwise return false
		return info.isSelfField
	end 'isSelfField'

	// Get the declared MaxonType for a variable. Callers check `contains(name)`
	// first, so a missing entry is an invariant violation.
	export function getType(name ByteArray) returns MaxonType
		let info = try vars.get(name) otherwise panic("Scope.getType: name '{String.from(name)}' not in scope — callers must check `contains` first")
		return info.varType
	end 'getType'

	// Clone hook: install a VarInfo verbatim, preserving its slot id. Does NOT
	// bump `nextSlot` — the caller sets it explicitly via `setNextSlot`.
	export function installVerbatim(info VarInfo)
		vars.upsert(info.name, value: info)
	end 'installVerbatim'

	// Clone hook: pin `nextSlot` to a chosen value.
	export function setNextSlot(slot VarSlot)
		nextSlot = slot
	end 'setNextSlot'

	// Open a fresh scope frame — O(1), one integer, no heap object.
	export function pushScope()
		frameMarks.push(declNames.count())
	end 'pushScope'

	// Close the current scope frame: drain its declarations out of `vars`, in REVERSE,
	// restoring each binding they displaced (see the header — reverse order is what
	// unwinds a name declared twice in one frame through its own shadow chain).
	// O(declarations-in-this-frame).
	//
	// THE OWNERSHIP HOOK LIVES HERE (M6): each iteration already holds the `VarInfo`
	// being torn down, so `ownedInFrame` classifies it in place and the Own tier emits
	// its `own.drop`/`own.release` at exactly this point. At M1 every binding is
	// `trivial`, so the classifier is always false and nothing is emitted.
	export function popScope()
		let mark = try frameMarks.pop() otherwise panic("Scope.popScope: frameMarks is empty (unmatched popScope)")
		while declNames.count() > mark 'drain'
			let name = try declNames.pop() otherwise panic("Scope.popScope: declNames.pop failed above the frame mark {mark}")
			let shadowed = try declShadowed.pop() otherwise panic("Scope.popScope: declShadowed.pop failed — it must stay parallel to declNames")

			// Drained in reverse, so `name` still resolves to the binding THIS declaration
			// installed — the one leaving scope, and the one a drop would be emitted for.
			let leaving = try vars.get(name) otherwise panic("Scope.popScope: '{String.from(name)}' is in the frame's declaration list but not in `vars` — the two fell out of lockstep")
			if not isTrivialOwnership(leaving.ownership) 'dropAtScopeExit'
				panic("Scope.popScope: binding '{String.from(name)}' leaves scope owning a value, but the Own tier that emits its drop is not built yet (M6) — no binding is non-trivial before then")
			end 'dropAtScopeExit'

			if shadowed 'restoreOuter'
				let outer = try shadowedInfos.pop() otherwise panic("Scope.popScope: shadowedInfos.pop failed — one entry per declaration marked as shadowing")
				vars.upsert(name, value: outer)
			end 'restoreOuter' else 'removeOnly'
				_ = vars.remove(name)
			end 'removeOnly'
		end 'drain'
	end 'popScope'
end 'Scope'

// A stack of `Scope`s — held by the parser as it detects closure captures (P1.5-A2a; see
// `Parser.closureCaptureScopes`). A `Scope` is a reference type, so this stack holds pointers to the
// live enclosing scopes a closure body is checked against, never copies of them.
export typealias ScopeArray = Array with Scope
